<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikawa Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikawa Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the eastern part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>. It was the home of the Matsudaira family, whose heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> spent part of his youth as a hostage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, then overlords of the province. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a>'s death in 1560 Ieyasu made himself master of Mikawa, the first base of the power that later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikawa-province.jpg" alt="Mikawa Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Mikawa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
